{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-post-js","path":"/posts/built-in-ai-in-chrome-summarizer-translator-language-detector-in-production/","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"html":"<p><span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n      style=\"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 1280px; \"\n    >\n      <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/00711/hero.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n    <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n    style=\"padding-bottom: 33.4375%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n  ></span>\n  <picture>\n          <source\n              srcset=\"/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/fb34c/hero.avif 320w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/646cd/hero.avif 768w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/8b485/hero.avif 1024w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/a73d6/hero.avif 1280w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/a7f9f/hero.avif 1920w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/fb732/hero.avif 2400w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n              type=\"image/avif\"\n            /><source\n              srcset=\"/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/cb523/hero.webp 320w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/cd067/hero.webp 768w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/53334/hero.webp 1024w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/4b075/hero.webp 1280w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/f3ff0/hero.webp 1920w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/4293a/hero.webp 2400w\"\n              sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n              type=\"image/webp\"\n            />\n          <source\n            srcset=\"/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/d4f27/hero.png 320w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/778aa/hero.png 768w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/658fc/hero.png 1024w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/26c69/hero.png 1280w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/7d442/hero.png 1920w,\n/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/00711/hero.png 2400w\"\n            sizes=\"(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px\"\n            type=\"image/png\"\n          />\n          <img\n            class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n            src=\"/static/4341da5d37d4def27f7ecf411983f6b0/26c69/hero.png\"\n            alt=\"hero\"\n            title=\"\"\n            loading=\"lazy\"\n            decoding=\"async\"\n            style=\"width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;\"\n          />\n        </picture>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>TL;DR; The Summarizer, Translator and Language Detector APIs are stable in Chrome since version 138. Treat them as a progressive enhancement: detect, check <code class=\"language-text\">availability()</code>, show the download, and always keep a fallback. 🎉</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>This is the second post of my series <em>AI on the web in 2026</em>. If you missed the first one, <a href=\"/posts/ai-on-the-web-in-2026-a-gde-map-of-what-shipped/\">the map of the year</a> explains how the series is organised.</p>\n<p>The task-based built-in AI APIs are the least glamorous part of the year, and probably the most useful. They don't chat. They do one job each: summarise text, translate it, or tell you which language it is in. And they do that on the user's device, without your server in the loop.</p>\n<p>In this article, we'll go through the pattern I use to put them in production: feature detection, availability checks, download monitoring, fallbacks, and the limits you need to know before you promise anything to your product team.</p>\n<h2>The Problem with \"just call the API\"</h2>\n<p>The happy path looks tiny. The real world is not. Before the first summary, you need to know that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The APIs run on desktop Chrome only: Windows 10 or 11, macOS 13+, Linux and Chromebook Plus. <strong>They don't work on Android or iOS.</strong></li>\n<li>The Summarizer uses a local language model. The device needs at least 22 GB free on the volume with the Chrome profile, plus a GPU with more than 4 GB of VRAM, or a CPU with 16 GB of RAM and 4 cores.</li>\n<li>The Translator and Language Detector use smaller expert models, but language packs still download on demand.</li>\n<li><code class=\"language-text\">create()</code> should be called after a user activation, and the APIs are not available in Web Workers.</li>\n<li>Cross-origin iframes need a permissions policy, like <code class=\"language-text\">allow=\"summarizer\"</code> or <code class=\"language-text\">allow=\"translator\"</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the question is never \"does it work?\". It's \"does it work <strong>for this user, right now</strong>?\".</p>\n<h2>The Solution: detect, check, create, fall back</h2>\n<p>Every one of these APIs follows the same shape. A global (<code class=\"language-text\">Summarizer</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">Translator</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">LanguageDetector</code>), a static <code class=\"language-text\">availability()</code> that returns <code class=\"language-text\">\"unavailable\"</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">\"downloadable\"</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">\"downloading\"</code> or <code class=\"language-text\">\"available\"</code>, and a static <code class=\"language-text\">create()</code> that accepts a <code class=\"language-text\">monitor</code> callback for the download.</p>\n<h4>1. Summarizer with download progress</h4>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"token function\">summarize</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token parameter\">text<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> onProgress</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token operator\">!</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'Summarizer'</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">in</span> self<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"token function\">serverSummary</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> options <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">type</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'key-points'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">format</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'markdown'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">length</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'medium'</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> availability <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> Summarizer<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">availability</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>options<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>availability <span class=\"token operator\">===</span> <span class=\"token string\">'unavailable'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"token function\">serverSummary</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">try</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> summarizer <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> Summarizer<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">create</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"token operator\">...</span>options<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"token literal-property property\">sharedContext</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'Product reviews from an online store'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n      <span class=\"token function\">monitor</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token parameter\">m</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n        m<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">addEventListener</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'downloadprogress'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token parameter\">e</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token operator\">=></span> <span class=\"token function\">onProgress</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>e<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>loaded<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n      <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n    <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> summarizer<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">summarize</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n      <span class=\"token literal-property property\">context</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> <span class=\"token string\">'Keep it neutral and short.'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span>\n    <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">catch</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>error<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    console<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">warn</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'On-device summary failed'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> error<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n    <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"token function\">serverSummary</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>As you can see, the built-in path is the enhancement and <code class=\"language-text\">serverSummary()</code> is the floor. That function can call your backend, a cloud model, or simply hide the feature. The important part is that the UI never depends on the local model existing.</p>\n<p>Keep in mind that <code class=\"language-text\">e.loaded</code> goes from <code class=\"language-text\">0</code> to <code class=\"language-text\">1</code>, so you can drive a progress bar straight from it. The options can't be changed after creation, so create a new summarizer when the user picks a different <code class=\"language-text\">type</code> (<code class=\"language-text\">key-points</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">tldr</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">teaser</code> or <code class=\"language-text\">headline</code>). For long outputs, <code class=\"language-text\">summarizeStreaming()</code> returns an async iterable you can render chunk by chunk, which pairs nicely with <a href=\"/posts/skeleton-loaders-for-llm-latency-ngx-skeleton-loader-for-streaming-uis/\">skeleton loaders for streaming UIs</a>.</p>\n<h4>2. Language Detector before the Translator</h4>\n<p>Translation needs a source language, and users don't tell you that. So I chain the two APIs:</p>\n<div class=\"gatsby-highlight\" data-language=\"js\"><pre class=\"language-js\"><code class=\"language-js\"><span class=\"token keyword\">async</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">function</span> <span class=\"token function\">translateToUserLanguage</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token parameter\">text<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> targetLanguage</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token operator\">!</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'LanguageDetector'</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">in</span> self<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token operator\">||</span> <span class=\"token operator\">!</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token string\">'Translator'</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">in</span> self<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> text<span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> detector <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> LanguageDetector<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">create</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">[</span>top<span class=\"token punctuation\">]</span> <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> detector<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">detect</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token operator\">!</span>top <span class=\"token operator\">||</span> top<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>confidence <span class=\"token operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"token number\">0.6</span> <span class=\"token operator\">||</span> top<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>detectedLanguage <span class=\"token operator\">===</span> targetLanguage<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span>\n    <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> text<span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span>\n\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> pair <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">{</span> <span class=\"token literal-property property\">sourceLanguage</span><span class=\"token operator\">:</span> top<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span>detectedLanguage<span class=\"token punctuation\">,</span> targetLanguage <span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">if</span> <span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span><span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> Translator<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">availability</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>pair<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token operator\">===</span> <span class=\"token string\">'unavailable'</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> text<span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">const</span> translator <span class=\"token operator\">=</span> <span class=\"token keyword\">await</span> Translator<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">create</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>pair<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n  <span class=\"token keyword\">return</span> translator<span class=\"token punctuation\">.</span><span class=\"token function\">translate</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">(</span>text<span class=\"token punctuation\">)</span><span class=\"token punctuation\">;</span>\n<span class=\"token punctuation\">}</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><code class=\"language-text\">detect()</code> returns a ranked list of <code class=\"language-text\">{ detectedLanguage, confidence }</code>, with confidence between <code class=\"language-text\">0.0</code> and <code class=\"language-text\">1.0</code>. The Chrome docs are honest about it: very short phrases and single words give low accuracy. The threshold above is my own choice; tune it against the languages your product cares about.</p>\n<h2>The privacy upside (and the fine print)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The best request is the one that never leaves the device.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The text you summarise or translate stays on the user's machine. For reviews, support tickets or any user-generated content, that is a real argument in a privacy review, and there is no per-call cost on your side.</p>\n<p>The Translator also protects users in a subtle way: it hides which language pairs are already downloaded. Until your site creates a translator for a pair, every pair looks <code class=\"language-text\">downloadable</code>. So don't build logic that assumes <code class=\"language-text\">available</code> means \"the user speaks this language\".</p>\n<p>The fine print: Summarizer output currently supports <code class=\"language-text\">en</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">ja</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">es</code>, <code class=\"language-text\">de</code> and <code class=\"language-text\">fr</code>. Outside those, fall back. And the first model download needs an unmetered connection, so don't trigger it on page load. Wait for a click that clearly says what is about to happen.</p>\n<h2>Shipping it safely</h2>\n<p>What made these APIs boring (in the good sense) for me:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gate the feature behind a flag.</strong> Roll out to a small percentage first; <a href=\"/posts/feature-flagging-gemini-features-in-angular-with-ngx-feature-toggle/\">feature flagging Gemini features</a> covers that in Angular.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure the three paths separately</strong>: on-device, fallback, and hidden. If most users hit the fallback, your backend is the real feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Never block the main flow on a download.</strong> Show progress, let the user keep working.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you want one API that switches between local and cloud for you, the next posts cover the <a href=\"/posts/structured-output-from-the-chrome-prompt-api-with-json-schema/\">Prompt API with JSON Schema</a> and <a href=\"/posts/hybrid-inference-with-firebase-ai-logic-on-device-first-cloud-fallback/\">hybrid inference with Firebase AI Logic</a>.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/built-in-apis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Built-in AI APIs overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/get-started\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Get started with built-in AI (hardware requirements)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/summarizer-api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Summarize with built-in AI</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/translator-api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Translation with built-in AI</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/language-detection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Language detection with built-in AI</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>That’s all for now</h3>\n<p>Built-in AI is not a replacement for your backend. It's a faster, private first option for the users who can run it.</p>\n<p>I hope you enjoyed this reading as much as I enjoyed writing it. Thank you so much for reading until the end and see you soon!</p>\n<p>🚀🚀🚀🚀🚀🚀</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Cya 👋</h3>","frontmatter":{"date":"November 23, 2026","path":"/posts/built-in-ai-in-chrome-summarizer-translator-language-detector-in-production/","title":"Built-in AI in Chrome — Summarizer, Translator and Language Detector in production","tags":["frontend","javascript","ai","chrome","web","performance"],"status":"active"},"timeToRead":4}},"pageContext":{"postPath":"/posts/built-in-ai-in-chrome-summarizer-translator-language-detector-in-production/"}},"staticQueryHashes":["1271460761","63159454"],"slicesMap":{}}